<ClinicNote slug="de-quervains-tenosynovitis">
  <p>Two tendons that move your thumb, abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, slide through a narrow tunnel on the thumb side of the wrist. When that tunnel's lining thickens, the tendons stop gliding smoothly and every pinch or twist rubs. It's most common in people in their 40s and 50s, more common in women, and often shows up in the weeks after having a baby.</p>
  <h2>How we check it</h2>
  <p>We start with how it began and what aggravates it, then examine the wrist and thumb. A simple test (tucking the thumb into the palm and gently tilting the wrist towards the little finger) usually reproduces the pain. Scans are rarely needed.</p>
  <h2>What helps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Changing how you grip, lift and carry for a while, so the tendons can settle</li>
    <li>A thumb splint, especially overnight</li>
    <li>Graded thumb and wrist strengthening once pain allows</li>
    <li>A corticosteroid injection from your GP or specialist when symptoms are significant</li>
  </ul>
  <blockquote><p>"These findings suggest that administration of CSI followed by 3 to 4 weeks immobilization should be considered as a first-line treatment for patients with DQT."</p><cite>Challoumas et al., JAMA Network Open, 2023 [2]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>That's why care here works alongside your GP. We'll help you manage load, fit a splint and rebuild strength, and we'll tell you if an injection referral is the smarter next step.</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Most patients with De Quervain's tenosynovitis do very well."</p><cite>American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, OrthoInfo [1]</cite></blockquote>

